{:db/id 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186073446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n![](images/77/77459b19bb1fd1f28e05e6f613e4e49af34595eae33eeeca584d65a289f7ea2c.svg)\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\n3v &= -2 \\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\n\tu &= 6\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186073997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:a